\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Factorization through an invariant requires fiber constancy}
\date{2026-10-30}

\p{Let #{X,C,Y} be sets and let #{c:X\to C} and #{r:X\to Y}
be functions. There is a function #{F:c(X)\to Y} with
#{r=F\circ c}, viewing #{c} as a map to its image, if and only if
##{c(x)=c(x')\ \Longrightarrow\ r(x)=r(x')}
for all #{x,x'\in X}. When it exists, #{F} is unique on #{c(X)}.
This is the set-level form of the fiber-constancy argument for
[finite-map pullback](fdip-0028); no finiteness is required here.}

\proof{
\p{If #{r=F\circ c}, equal inputs to #{F} give equal outputs.
Conversely, assign to #{z\in c(X)} the common value of #{r}
on its nonempty fiber. The condition makes that value well-defined
and gives #{F(c(x))=r(x)}. Every element of #{c(X)} is reached,
so this identity also gives uniqueness. This includes the empty
image, on which there is exactly one function to #{Y}.}
}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{Function.FactorsThrough} is the displayed
fiber-constancy condition. Its \leanref{Function.factorsThrough_iff}
extends to the entire codomain #{C} when #{Y} is nonempty;
the image formulation above needs no such extra assumption.}

\p{Apply this to any set of representatives of the rational
[enriched objects](fdip-0041), first taking #{c(\mathcal B)} to be
the rational quadratic-isometry class of #{q}
and #{r(\mathcal B)=R(\mathcal B)}. Both are unchanged by the
declared arrows. A pair of isometric forms with unequal trace
ratios excludes every set-level recovery rule from that isometry
class on a domain containing the pair. It consequently excludes
recovery from any invariant determined solely by that class,
including the Clifford algebra's isomorphism class and the
[quadratic center character](fdip-0049) #{\chi_q}. In particular,
a fixed function of #{L(2,\chi_D)}, or of any fixed collection
of values determined solely by the same character, cannot
recover #{R} there. The [two rationally isometric fibers](fdip-0043) provide
such a pair.}

\p{The criterion asserts neither regularity nor continuity of
#{F}. Even fiber constancy would not supply those properties,
a choice of a physical observable, or a physical parameter law.
The obstruction concerns invariants of #{q} alone and this
independently defined trace ratio. It makes no assertion about
arithmetic data that also retain the reference pairing and mass.}
